---
const docsUrl = "https://github.com/a2f0/tearleads/blob/main/docs";
const primitives = [
  {
    purpose: "Document & blob records",
    mechanism: "AES-256-GCM + HKDF-SHA256",
    detail:
      "Authenticated encryption with a separate derived key for each content record.",
  },
  {
    purpose: "Recipient key delivery",
    mechanism: "ML-KEM-1024 + AES-256-GCM",
    detail:
      "Wraps container keys for users and groups. Content keys are wrapped under container keys with AES-GCM.",
  },
  {
    purpose: "Digital signatures",
    mechanism: "ML-DSA-87",
    detail:
      "Authenticates signed policies, access events, and content-write headers.",
  },
  {
    purpose: "Local key separation",
    mechanism: "HKDF-SHA256",
    detail:
      "Derives purpose-specific storage keys from the wrapped local account root.",
  },
];
---

<section class="security-section" id="cryptography" aria-labelledby="cryptography-title">
  <p class="security-eyebrow">03 / Cryptography</p>
  <h2 id="cryptography-title">Different keys for different jobs.</h2>
  <p class="security-section-intro">
    Content encryption, recipient key delivery, signatures, and local storage
    each have a distinct role in the protocol.
  </p>
  <div class="security-crypto-table-wrap">
    <table class="security-crypto-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Cryptographic primitives and their roles in Tearleads</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Mechanism &amp; role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {primitives.map((primitive) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{primitive.purpose}</th>
            <td><strong>{primitive.mechanism}</strong><p>{primitive.detail}</p></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <details class="security-detail">
    <summary>How local storage and optional PIN protection work</summary>
    <div>
      <p>
        The local keyring stores a wrapped account root and derives keys for
        encrypted SQLite, local blobs, and identity persistence. Browser data
        persistence uses the origin-private file system (OPFS); the keyring
        manifest and wrapping key normally live in IndexedDB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Protection depends on the host keystore. The browser implementation
        persists a Web Crypto key; the WebView variant defaults to exported key
        bytes in IndexedDB for compatibility. This is not a universal
        hardware-backed keychain guarantee.
      </p>
      <p>
        Optional PIN support adds a PBKDF2-SHA256 / AES-GCM wrapper around the
        host-wrapped root. Reopening then requires both the host key and PIN.
        The default browser keyring does not require a PIN, and a local PIN
        does not change remote sharing permissions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="security-section" id="trust-boundaries" aria-labelledby="trust-title">
  <p class="security-eyebrow">04 / Trust &amp; limits</p>
  <h2 id="trust-title">The boundaries are part of the design.</h2>
  <p class="security-section-intro">
    These properties assume an honest, uncompromised client, authentic identity
    keys, and working cryptographic primitives. The API and its storage may be
    compromised; some behaviors still cannot be detected or prevented.
  </p>
  <dl class="security-limits">
    <div>
      <dt>First contact establishes trust</dt>
      <dd>
        Clients pin the first accepted identity bundle and reject later changes.
        A server that substitutes a key on that first lookup can still establish
        a false identity. A fingerprint check alone does not prove who owns it.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Checkpoints need a remembered view</dt>
      <dd>
        A new device without a checkpoint can receive old but valid signed
        history. Signatures alone do not prove that a view is the newest or that
        all devices see the same view. Independent witnessing or gossip is
        needed for stronger split-view resistance.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Encryption does not ensure availability</dt>
      <dd>
        The service can withhold ciphertext, policies, or key envelopes and
        refuse requests. When required verification material is missing, the
        operation cannot safely proceed.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>An unlocked device can read content</dt>
      <dd>
        Local encryption does not protect against malicious code running in an
        unlocked client or a compromised device. Anyone who obtains the recovery
        phrase, including its QR representation, can recover that identity.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <details class="security-detail">
    <summary>Revocation, nested containers, and historical access</summary>
    <div>
      <p>
        Revocation cannot retract previously learned keys or downloaded content.
        Rotating a parent container does not automatically rekey every descendant;
        refreshing a descendant's signed access history does not itself replace
        its encryption keys or promise confidentiality from a former member.
      </p>
      <p>
        A revoked signer working with a compromised server may still present
        records under valid historical authority to a device that has not seen
        newer signed state. Verifying that authority does not prove when the
        record was actually created.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current container keys also unlock retained predecessor keys so current
        members can read history. Compromise of a current container key therefore
        exposes that retained history. These limits matter when evaluating access
        removal and the impact of a compromised key.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <aside class="security-sources" aria-labelledby="security-sources-title">
    <p class="security-eyebrow">Go deeper</p>
    <h3 id="security-sources-title">Inspect the protocol.</h3>
    <p>
      This page summarizes the implementation. The technical documents describe
      the verification rules, assumptions, and remaining gaps in more detail.
    </p>
    <ul role="list">
      <li><a href={`${docsUrl}/security-guarantees.md`}>Security guarantees <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li>
      <li><a href={`${docsUrl}/keying-design.md`}>Keying design <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li>
      <li><a href={`${docsUrl}/protocol-specification.md`}>Protocol specification <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</section>
